// Darwin's `gcThreadToken` provider (#3420). See ../gcthread.bit for why this
// exists as a paired module now and what the split costs on every target,
// Darwin included.
//
// Arm64 payload only, byte-for-byte what used to live directly in
// ../gcthread.bit before the split — no x64-macos target exists (the only
// Darwin target this compiler ships is aarch64-macos), so there is no x64
// payload to carry here. `compiler/parserasm.bit`'s `result` directive still
// requires a nominal `x64 <reg>` alongside `arm64 <reg>` — that register name
// is never reached by codegen for an aarch64-macos build (`arm64compile.bit`'s
// `emitAsm` reads only the arm64 fields of the asm node) and is kept as
// `rax` purely so this asm block's shape matches its Linux/Windows twins.
//
// See ../gcthread.bit's header for the full argument (measured on hardware,
// not read off an ISA manual) for the dual probe below: TPIDRRO_EL0 first
// (Darwin's own per-thread pointer), falling back to TPIDR_EL0 (unused on
// Darwin, but read second in case a build ever runs where it isn't), masked
// because TPIDRRO_EL0's low 3 bits carry a CPU number on this OS.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token") fn gcThreadToken(): int {
  return asm { arm64 { 0xD53BD060, 0xB5000040, 0xD53BD040, 0x927DF000 } result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int }
}
